# tests/backrefs/d27/interactions.rxt -- D27 blinded acceptance corpus,
# module `backrefs`, cross-module interactions: a REFERENCED group under
# a possessive/atomic quantifier (module `atomic-groups`, which IS built
# per the registry), and a backreference beside a plain assertion
# (module `assertions`).
#
# Per the differential gate principle (docs/testing.md, "The differential
# gate principle"): enable exactly the modules the cell exercises, no
# more -- `features backrefs,atomic-groups` and `features
# backrefs,assertions` respectively, never a blanket feature list.
#
# Every measurement below is against real libpcre2 10.46 (br_oracle.py).
# Nothing here has been run against build/pcrec (module unbuilt).

# --- A group made possessive (`a*+`, PCRE2's own spelling of
# `(?>a*)`) is still an ordinary capturing group for reference purposes:
# once it has committed to its span, `\1` replays exactly that span, with
# no interaction between the possessive commit and the later backref
# other than the span being fixed early (no backtrack can revise it). ---
# pcre2-only: python's possessive-quantifier support diverges from PCRE2
# in other ways (docs/testing.md's brace-possessive note) and this
# specific combination with a backreference has no python equivalent
# construct to compare against cleanly
pattern ^(a*+)b\1a$
features backrefs,atomic-groups
m "aabaaa" 0 6
gp 1 0 2
n "baa"

# --- The mirror shape with `++`: same possessive-then-replay contract,
# one more layer removed from the exact `*+` case above. ---
# pcre2-only: see note above
pattern ^(a++)b\1a$
features backrefs,atomic-groups
m "aabaaa" 0 6
gp 1 0 2

# --- An explicit atomic group `(?>...)` wrapping the referenced capture
# itself. Same contract as the possessive-quantifier forms, spelled the
# other way. ---
# pcre2-only: see note above
pattern ^(?>(a+))b\1a$
features backrefs,atomic-groups
m "aabaaa" 0 6
gp 1 0 2

# --- A backreference beside a plain zero-width assertion: `\B` (NOT a
# word boundary) between a captured word character and its own replay is
# necessarily true (replaying the same word-character text can never
# cross a word/non-word transition against itself), so this always
# succeeds when the group is non-empty and adjacent to its own replay. ---
# pcre2-only: python's \B has the same semantics here, but the point of
# this cell is the MODULE gate (assertions) interacting with backrefs,
# which isn't independently python-checkable as a *combination fact*
pattern (a)\B\1
features backrefs,assertions
m "aa" 0 2
gp 1 0 1

# --- The complementary case: `\b` (a real word boundary) placed BETWEEN
# a group and its own replay of word-character text can never succeed
# for the same reason `\B` above always does -- replaying identical word
# characters can't itself be a word/non-word transition. ---
# pcre2-only: see note above
pattern ^(a)\b\1$
features backrefs,assertions
n "aa"

# --- `\b` placed AFTER the replay (at the true end-of-word boundary)
# succeeds normally -- the assertion and the backreference don't
# interact at all here, which is the expected, uninteresting case
# confirming the module combination doesn't break ordinary use. ---
# pcre2-only: see note above
pattern ^(a)\1\b$
features backrefs,assertions
m "aa" 0 2
gp 1 0 1
